---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import SeaBreak from '../../components/SeaBreak.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import CTABand from '../../components/CTABand.astro';
import { celestial, ocean } from '../../config/images';
const crumbs = [
  { label: 'Explore Astrology', href: '/explore/' },
  { label: 'Lunar Nodes', href: '/explore/lunar-nodes/' },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="The Lunar Nodes, the North and South Node axis"
  description="The lunar nodes are the two points where the Moon's orbit crosses the ecliptic. Learn how astrologers interpret the nodal axis, how it relates to eclipses, and why it is better read as a relationship than two labels."
  path="/explore/lunar-nodes/"
  breadcrumbs={crumbs}
>
  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={crumbs} />
  <section class="hero hero--split">
    <div class="container">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Explore Astrology</p>
        <h1>The lunar nodes</h1>
        <p class="lede">They are not planets at all, but two mathematical points created by the crossing of two orbital planes. I like that about them: something physically invisible can still describe a relationship in the geometry.</p>
      </div>
      <img class="hero__img" src={ocean.whaleTailStars.src} srcset={ocean.whaleTailStars.srcset} sizes="100vw" alt={ocean.whaleTailStars.alt} width={ocean.whaleTailStars.width} height={ocean.whaleTailStars.height} style="object-position: center 76%;" fetchpriority="high" />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--alt">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="prose flow" style="margin-bottom: var(--space-6);">
        <p class="eyebrow">One axis, three layers</p>
        <h2>North Node ↔ South Node</h2>
        <p>The nodes only make sense together. Before assigning meaning to either end, keep three different kinds of information separate.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="node-axis" aria-label="North and South Node teaching axis"><div><strong>South Node</strong><span>One end of the axis</span></div><span class="axis-line" aria-hidden="true">↔</span><div><strong>North Node</strong><span>The opposite end</span></div></div>
      <div class="grid grid--3" style="margin-top: var(--space-5);">
        <article class="card"><p class="eyebrow">1 · Astronomy</p><h3>What they are</h3><p>Two mathematical points where the Moon's orbital plane crosses the ecliptic.</p></article>
        <article class="card"><p class="eyebrow">2 · Interpretation</p><h3>What astrologers make of them</h3><p>Different traditions assign symbolic meaning to the axis. Those meanings are interpretation, not astronomical fact.</p></article>
        <article class="card"><p class="eyebrow">3 · Personal context</p><h3>Where the axis lands</h3><p>Signs, houses, aspects, birth-time reliability, and lived experience determine how specific the symbolism can become.</p></article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container prose flow">
      <h2>What they physically are</h2>
      <p>The Moon's orbit is tilted relative to the ecliptic. The two places where those planes intersect are the <strong>North Node</strong> and <strong>South Node</strong>. They are always opposite one another, so I find it more useful to think of them as one axis than as two separate placements.</p>
      <p>The nodes move backward through the zodiac over an approximately 18.6-year cycle. They are also central to the geometry of <a href="/explore/eclipses/">eclipses</a>: a solar or lunar eclipse occurs when a new or full moon falls close enough to this nodal axis.</p>
      <h2>How astrologers read them</h2>
      <p>Many modern astrologers interpret the South Node as familiar territory and the North Node as less familiar development. That language can be useful as long as it does not turn into a moral ranking in which one end of the axis is “bad” and the other is the destination you are supposed to reach.</p>
      <p>I prefer the relationship between them. The South Node can describe capacities, habits, or ways of operating that come more readily. The North Node can point toward qualities that ask for more conscious development. But the two work together. Growth rarely requires throwing away what you already know how to do.</p>
      <p>That is the larger lesson I keep finding in charts: opposites are often not problems to solve by choosing one side. They are relationships to learn how to hold.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <SeaBreak image={celestial.moth} />

  <section class="section section--sea">
    <div class="container prose flow">
      <h2>Signs, houses, and aspects</h2>
      <p>The node's sign describes one layer of the symbolism; the house shows where that axis falls in the life, and that house depends on your birth time. Aspects from planets add still more information. A nodal placement becomes more useful as context accumulates, not as the label becomes more dramatic.</p>
      <h2>The collective layer</h2>
      <p>Because the nodes remain on the same sign axis for many months, large groups of people share the same nodal signs. That is another reason sign-only interpretations cannot be very personal. The distinction comes from where the axis falls in an individual chart and what it connects with there.</p>
      <p>Related: <a href="/explore/eclipses/">eclipses</a>, <a href="/explore/transits/">transits and timing</a>, and <a href="/explore/personal-purpose/">what a chart can suggest about direction</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <CTABand heading="Want to follow the nodal axis into the sky itself?" body="Eclipses are where the nodes become visible in the larger cycle. Start there, then use Current Sky to see when those patterns are active now." buttonLabel="Explore Eclipses" buttonHref="/explore/eclipses/" secondaryLabel="See Current Sky" secondaryHref="/current-sky/" />
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .node-axis { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr auto 1fr; gap: var(--space-4); align-items: center; max-width: 42rem; margin-inline: auto; }
  .node-axis div { display: grid; gap: var(--space-1); padding: var(--space-5); text-align: center; border: 1px solid var(--color-border); border-radius: var(--radius-lg); background: var(--color-surface); }
  .axis-line { font-size: 2rem; font-weight: 700; }
  @media (max-width: 560px) { .node-axis { grid-template-columns: 1fr; } .axis-line { transform: rotate(90deg); justify-self: center; } }
</style>